{-
   Taking ownership out of borrowed storage and writing a value back into it.

   `acc = acc ++ part` through a `&` is the shape this is about, and it was refused outright: a move
   whose place is rooted in a borrow was rejected on sight, with no reference to what the body did
   next. The rule now asks one program point later. The storage is emptied, something is written
   back, and only then does the frame let go of the reference - so at every point the caller can
   observe, the storage holds a value.

   Two things make the window between the two unobservable, and both are properties of this
   language rather than assumptions. A `&` is exclusive while it is live, so no second reader exists
   to look into it; and nothing unwinds, so a failed check aborts the process rather than running
   the owner's teardown over a hole on the way out. Take either away and the rule goes with it.

   The reason it is worth a fixture rather than a paragraph is that the same body has to be accepted
   in two shapes that look nothing alike underneath. Written against a `&` parameter, `acc` is a
   local this frame has a slot for and the existing ownership lattice already had a row for it.
   Written inside a `for`, the loop body is lifted into a continuation and `acc` becomes a field of
   an environment the continuation is handed a *pointer* to - a place with no local behind it at
   all, which is the case the second lattice exists for. Adding a `for` to a working function used
   to turn its remaining lines into a barrage of errors about storage it had not stopped owning.

   Every group below also proves the *value* survived, not only that the program compiled. A write
   through a borrow releases what it replaces, and if it did that here it would release what the
   move had already handed away - the double free the old refusal was standing in for. A length is
   what catches it: a released buffer reused would answer something other than what was appended.
-}

import Test

data BorrowUpdateHeld {name: String, tally: Array(Int)}

fn borrowUpdateCounted(n: Int) -> Array(Int):
    let &out = [] :: [Int]
    let &i = 0
    while i < n:
        push(out, i)
        i = i + 1
    return out

fn borrowUpdateSize(xs: Array(Int)) -> Int = truncate(length(xs)) :: Int
fn width(s: String) -> Int = truncate(length(s)) :: Int

iter fn each(xs: [String]) -> 'String:
    let &i = 0 :: Size
    while i < length(xs):
        yield xs[i]
        i = i + 1

-- The `&` parameter, which is the tier with a local behind it. The move marks the slot, the write
-- fills it again, and the state that survives to the `ret` is the second of the two.
fn append(&target: String, ->amount: String) -> {}:
    target = target ++ amount

@test fn throughAParameter() -> {}:
    let &->acc = "ab" :: String
    append(acc, "cd")
    append(acc, "ef")

    checkEqual(width(acc), 6)
    checkEqual(acc, "abcdef")

-- The same statement written where the compiler can see both ends of it, rather than across a call.
fn inTheSameFrame(&acc: String) -> {}:
    acc = acc ++ "cd"

@test fn withoutACall() -> {}:
    let &->acc = "ab" :: String
    inTheSameFrame(acc)

    checkEqual(acc, "abcd")

-- The tier this was really about: a `for` body, where `acc` is a field of a heap environment the
-- lifted continuation holds a raw pointer to. The two loads of that field have to be recognized as
-- one piece of storage or the write back looks like a write to something else.
@test fn overALoop() -> {}:
    let &->acc = "" :: String
    let parts = ["ab", "cd", "ef"] :: [String]

    for p in each(parts):
        acc = acc ++ copy(p)

    checkEqual(width(acc), 6)
    checkEqual(acc, "abcdef")

-- A container rather than a scalar, so that a released buffer answers a wrong length instead of a
-- wrong string, and a borrow *of a field* rather than of the whole record.
--
-- The borrow is taken at the call site and not inside the callee, which is the pre-existing rule
-- about partial moves rather than anything to do with this one: `h.tally = h.tally ++ extra` inside
-- a body holding `&h` moves one member out of a value and leaves the rest, and a slot half given
-- away needs a drop flag per field. `&h.tally` hands over the member's own storage, and the move is
-- whole again.
fn growTally(&tally: Array(Int), ->extra: Array(Int)) -> {}:
    tally = tally ++ extra

@test fn throughAField() -> {}:
    let &->h = BorrowUpdateHeld {name: "h", tally: borrowUpdateCounted(3)}
    growTally(h.tally, borrowUpdateCounted(5))

    checkEqual(borrowUpdateSize(h.tally), 8)
    checkEqual(h.name, "h")

-- Emptied and filled on both arms of a branch, which is the join the lattice has to get right: the
-- state reaching the `ret` is Owned from either side, and neither arm owes a drop for what it
-- overwrites, because there was nothing there to release.
fn pickOne(&acc: String, longer: Bool) -> {}:
    let ->old = acc
    if longer:
        acc = old ++ "xy"
    else:
        acc = old

@test fn acrossABranch() -> {}:
    let &->a = "ab" :: String
    let &->b = "ab" :: String
    pickOne(a, True)
    pickOne(b, False)

    checkEqual(a, "abxy")
    checkEqual(b, "ab")

-- Twice through the same borrow with a write between them, which is what a loop body does on its
-- second iteration and what the double-move check has to let past.
fn twiceOver(&acc: String) -> {}:
    acc = acc ++ "cd"
    acc = acc ++ "ef"

@test fn repeatedly() -> {}:
    let &->acc = "ab" :: String
    twiceOver(acc)

    checkEqual(acc, "abcdef")
